{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the only son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father was assassinated in 1152 and Raymond III became count at the age of twelve. Because he was too young to rule he was placed under the guardianship of his mother and later under the care of the Knights Templar. During his minority the County of Tripoli was governed by regents and the Templars played a major role in maintaining stability. Raymond III was known for his cautious nature and his deep involvement in the politics of the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a central figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem after the death of King Amalric I in 1174. He was named regent for the young King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was chosen due to his noble lineage and his experience in governance. He served as regent from 1174 to 1176 and again from 1183 to 1185. During his regencies he faced constant challenges from powerful nobles and the military orders. He often clashed with the Hospitallers and the Templars over control of territory and influence. Raymond also opposed the rise of Guy of Lusignan who eventually became king. His political maneuvering was aimed at preserving the stability of the kingdom but his decisions were frequently criticized by contemporaries.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III was deeply involved in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He had long been at odds with Guy of Lusignan and refused to support his aggressive campaigns against Saladin. Raymond even made a truce with Saladin which angered many in the kingdom. When Guy decided to march to relieve the siege of Tiberias Raymond refused to join the army. His absence weakened the Crusader forces. After the disastrous defeat at Hattin Raymond was captured by Saladin and held in captivity. He was released in 1188 but died shortly after in 1187. His death marked the end of a long line of counts who had defended Tripoli for over a century. Raymond III is remembered as a cautious and intelligent ruler whose efforts to maintain peace were often misunderstood by his peers. His legacy is tied to the decline of the Crusader states in the Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -4.625213477993384e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.002193308901041746}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -2.288792165927589e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.005234109237790108}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.004377663601189852}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.00652279332280159}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0804188996553421}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -8.34461570775602e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0011762612266466022}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0001754606782924384}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -4.8636207793606445e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.3868855834007263}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0015063855098560452}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.1205610462639015e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "7